      "content": "The timbre of a transverse flute is heavily influenced by its predominantly sine-wave-like fundamental and the presence of distinct, well-separated odd-numbered harmonics. In contrast, a clarinet often features a richer, more complex combination of both even and odd harmonics, leading to a fundamentally different, often more nasal or reedy sound. This spectral balance dictates how the flute resonates, yielding its bright, pure, and airy quality compared to the clarinet's darker, more complex overtone structure.",
